          ":question/id": "8f2cac98-d46c-5a68-832a-abb863407c09",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-65502",
          ":question/problem": "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"
