          ":question/id": "31d126d7-a9c2-531e-862e-71990d0a9843",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-274500",
          ":question/problem": "Express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
        },
        {
          ":db/id": 17592186292742,
