          ":question/id": "2fbd7b19-3db6-511d-9b76-1b94d19c3553",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-332521",
          ":question/problem": "Express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(x+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(x+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(x+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
        },
