          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-332522",
          ":question/problem": "Express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(x+\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(x+\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(x+\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =1\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+0\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
