          ":question/id": "50e696a4-2e9c-5fba-a55e-51a9e433151b",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-332526",
          ":question/problem": "Express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
        },
