    "lessons": [
      {
        ":activity/expected-seconds": 840.0,
        ":activity/first-step": {
          ":db/id": 17592186060512
        },
        ":activity/id": "17339bb9-1659-5064-a793-2d84ac6e8242",
        ":activity/scope": {
          ":db/id": 17592186048319
        },
        ":activity/steps": [
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186060512,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186060891,
              ":tutorial/content": "The following two identities are known as the **sum and difference formulas for cosine**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x\\,+\\,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y\\,\\,-\\,\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}(x\\,-\\,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y\\,\\,+\\,\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove these results at the end of the lesson.\n\nNotice that the signs are somewhat counterintuitive. The sum formula for cosine contains a minus sign $({\\color{red}{-}}),$ while the difference formula contains a plus sign $({\\color{blue}{+}}).$ \n\nThese identities can help calculate the cosine of an angle that can be expressed as the sum or difference of two special angles.\n\nFor example, we can use the sum formula to find the exact value of $\\cos(105^\\circ).$ First, we write\n\n$$\n105^\\circ = {\\color{blue}45^\\circ} + {\\color{red}60^\\circ}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula for cosine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}{105}^{∘} & =\\operatorname{cos}({45}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}{45}^{∘}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}60-\\operatorname{sin}{45}^{∘}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}60 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
              ":tutorial/id": "a9114a20-3220-5a44-b0ed-4e405d4317a4",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 2563,
              ":tutorial/title": "Introduction"
            },
            ":step/id": "2afe3115-494d-5055-8e96-efb5e53aeb5e",
            ":step/math-academy-id": 11766,
            ":step/next": {
              ":db/id": 17592186060514
            }
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186060513,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186059665,
              ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
                ":db/id": 17592186059496,
                ":question/id": "1d8679d3-8922-5471-811f-60c8c41c853a",
                ":question/math-academy-id": "e-3522",
                ":question/problem": "Express $\\cos\\left( x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right)$ in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x.$",
                ":question/worked-solution": "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u = x$ and $v = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
              },
              ":knowledge-point/id": "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64",
