              ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
                ":db/id": 17592186059628,
                ":question/id": "b643e820-52c3-5c08-a099-aecbed7b2b2a",
                ":question/math-academy-id": "e-9991",
                ":question/problem": "Express $\\cos(x - 60^\\circ)$ in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x.$",
                ":question/worked-solution": "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos{u}\\cos{v} + \\sin{u}\\sin{v}\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u = x$ and $v = 60^\\circ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{60}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
              },
              ":knowledge-point/id": "c6a58962-cb06-519a-8c64-0dfa3b9c14d7",
              ":knowledge-point/level-coverage": [
                {
                  ":db/id": 17592186045485
                },
                {
                  ":db/id": 17592186045487
                }
              ],
              ":knowledge-point/title": "Simplifying Expressions in Degrees Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"
            },
            ":step/id": "8279c645-73d9-562a-a923-1077bbc0cf0e",
            ":step/math-academy-id": 37636,
            ":step/next": {
              ":db/id": 17592186060513
            }
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186060804,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186060864,
              ":tutorial/content": "Let's now prove the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(A-B) = \\cos A\\cos B + \\sin A\\sin B\n$$\n\nConsider two points $P$ and $Q$ on the unit circle whose central angles have measures $A$ and $B,$ respectively, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5ed2cf9e528e7ec6762875f3dfaf211aba656e6b39025109315539a7dd4b013a.png)\n\nSince $P$ and $Q$ lie on the unit circle, their coordinates are\n\n$$\nP(\\cos A, \\sin A), \\qquad Q(\\cos B, \\sin B).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the squared distance $PQ^2$ between $P$ and $Q$ can be found using the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P{Q}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{cos}A-\\operatorname{cos}B)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}A-\\operatorname{sin}B)}^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}A-2\\operatorname{cos}A\\operatorname{cos}B+{cos}^{2}B+{sin}^{2}A-2\\operatorname{sin}A\\operatorname{sin}B+{sin}^{2}B \\\\  & =({cos}^{2}A+{sin}^{2}A)+({cos}^{2}B+{sin}^{2}B)-2(\\operatorname{cos}A\\operatorname{cos}B+\\operatorname{sin}A\\operatorname{sin}B)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\cos^2 B + \\sin^2 B = \\cos^2 A + \\sin^2 A = 1\n$$\n\nfor all $A$ and $B$ by the Pythagorean identity, our expression for $PQ^2$ simplifies as\n\n$$\nPQ^2 = 1+1 - 2(\\cos A\\cos B + \\sin A\\sin B)\n$$\n\ni.e., \n\n$$\nPQ^2 = 2 - 2(\\cos A\\cos B + \\sin A\\sin B).\n$$\n\nWe can also calculate $PQ^2$ by applying the law of cosines to $\\triangle POQ{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P{Q}^{2}={1}^{2}+{1}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}(A-B)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\nPQ^2 = 2 - 2\\cos(A-B).\n$$\n\nEquating the two expressions for $PQ^2,$ we get\n\n$$\n2 - 2(\\cos A\\cos B + \\sin A\\sin B) = 2 - 2\\cos(A-B)\n$$\n\n \n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\cos(A-B) = \\cos A\\cos B + \\sin A\\sin B\n$$\n\nas required.",
              ":tutorial/id": "98298911-e456-5b6c-92f0-315fe2bfca4a",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 13282,
              ":tutorial/title": "Deriving the Difference Formula"
            },
            ":step/id": "00e554fc-e6f5-5f65-bed6-576c4f6d26f7",
            ":step/math-academy-id": 461993,
            ":step/next": {
              ":db/id": 17592186060805
            }
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186060805,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186060865,
              ":tutorial/content": "We have shown how to derive the difference formula\n\n$$\n\\cos(A-B) = \\cos A\\cos B + \\sin A\\sin B.\n$$\n\nWe can derive the sum formula for cosine by simply replacing $B$ with $-B,$ and using the evenness/oddness identities for cosine and sine, respectively:\n\n$$\n\\cos(-B) = \\cos B, \\qquad \\sin(-B) = -\\sin B\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into our difference formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(A-(-B))=\\operatorname{cos}A\\operatorname{cos}(-B)+\\operatorname{sin}A\\operatorname{sin}(-B)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\cos(A+B) = \\cos A\\cos B - \\sin A\\sin B.\n$$",
              ":tutorial/id": "67134b4c-9f78-59b0-b0e7-42049c393c9c",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 13283,
              ":tutorial/title": "Deriving the Sum Formula"
            },
            ":step/id": "5e38ce17-dfbd-58ac-95c8-c7020d6e1b3c",
            ":step/math-academy-id": 461994
          }
        ],
        ":activity/title": "The Sum and Difference Formulas for Cosine",
